The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2042 (2012), 2043 (2012), 2118 (2013), 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2175 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2258 (2015), 2268 (2016), 2286 (2016), 2332 (2016), 2336 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), 2449 (2018), 2504 (2020), 2533 (2020), and 2585 (2021), and its Presidential Statements of 3 August 2011 (S/PRST/2011/16), 21 March 2012 (S/PRST/2012/6), 5 April 2012 (S/PRST/2012/10), 2 October 2013 (S/PRST/2013/15), 24 April 2015 (S/PRST/2015/10), 17 August 2015 (S/PRST/2015/15), and 8 October 2019 (S/PRST/2019/12),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Recognizing that the humanitarian situation in the Syrian Arab Republic continues to be of grave concern, and recalling that humanitarian assistance must be delivered in accordance with the United Nations guiding principles of humanitarian emergency assistance, including full respect for the sovereignty of the Syrian Arab Republic,

Emphasizing that humanitarian aid delivery should increasingly be conducted through cross-line modalities from within the territory of the Syrian Arab Republic and that the cross-border mechanism was conceived as an exceptional and temporary measure,

Recognizing that humanitarian action must not be limited to emergency relief and should actively encompass early recovery projects, including water, sanitation, health, education, electricity, and shelter, in order to restore basic essential services and create conditions conducive to the safe, dignified, and voluntary return of refugees and internally displaced persons,

Expressing concern over the negative humanitarian consequences of unilateral coercive measures imposed on the Syrian Arab Republic, which impede the delivery of basic goods, medicine, medical equipment, and early recovery assistance,

1. Decides to extend the decisions in paragraphs 2 and 3 of Security Council resolution 2165 (2014) for a period of six months, that is, until 10 January 2023, only for the border crossing at Bab al-Hawa, with an extension of an additional six months, that is, until 10 July 2023, subject to the issuance of a separate resolution by the Security Council;

2. Calls upon all Member States to respond with practical steps to address the urgent needs of the Syrian people, and welcomes all efforts and initiatives to broaden humanitarian activities, including early recovery projects in water, sanitation, health, education, electricity essential to restore basic services, and shelter, undertaken by the United Nations, the International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC), and other humanitarian organizations, and calls upon all relevant parties and international donors to support and fund these vital projects without preconditions or politicization;

3. Encourages further efforts to improve and significantly expand cross-line deliveries of humanitarian assistance to all parts of Syria, and calls on all relevant parties to ensure the unhindered delivery of such assistance without discrimination;

4. Requests the Secretary-General to brief the Council monthly and to provide a report on a regular basis, at least every 60 days, on the implementation of resolutions 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2258 (2015), 2332 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), 2449 (2018), 2504 (2020), 2533 (2020), 2585 (2021) and this resolution, with a specific focus on progress in cross-line access, early recovery initiatives, and enhanced transparency of operations;

5. Requests the Secretary-General to include in his reports an assessment of the impact of unilateral coercive measures on the humanitarian situation and the delivery of humanitarian aid in the Syrian Arab Republic;

6. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

